WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.000
<b>Calpis theater</b>

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Subs: English

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:14.000
S

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:14.000
<b><i>SILVER ZERO
 SUBS</i></b>

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:14.000
http://silverzerosubs.blogspot.com/

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:33.830
Original story by Johanna Spyri

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:33.830
<b>Alps no Shoujo Heidi</b>

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.090
<u>Why do I hear</u>

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.090
Kuchibue ha naze

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.250
<u>the whistling from far away?</u>

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.250
Tooku made kikoeru no

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.460
<u>Why is that cloud</u>

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.460
Ano kumo ha naze

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.600
<u>waiting for me?</u>

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.600
Watashi wo matteru no

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.800
<u>Tell me, Grandfather.</u>

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.800
Oshiete ojiisan

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.690
<u>Tell me, Grandfather.</u>

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.690
Oshiete ojiisan

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:59.380
<u>Oh tell me, the fir trees of the Alm.</u>

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:59.380
Oshiete arumu no momi no ki yo

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:26.930
To Grandmother's House

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.160
<i>It's the morning after Peter came to visit.</i>

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.200
Good morning, Grandfather!

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.460
Good morning, Joseph.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:43.880
Good morning!

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.050
Good morning. You woke up early today.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.590
That's because I don't want to be late.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.830
Late? Where are you going?

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.160
To Peter's house.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.190
His grandmother invited me.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.370
It's snowing too much, it's impossible to go.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.190
<i>She listens to Grandfather and
gives up on going that day,</i>

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.980
<i>but Heidi can't get the idea of
visiting grandmother's home</i>

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.660
<i>out of her head.</i>

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.750
<i>Since grandmother has invited her,</i>

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.940
<i>she feels she absolutely has to go.</i>

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.810
Grandfather, I have to go.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.670
Grandmother is waiting.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.220
It's still snowing.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.610
<i>Five days later, the snow stops falling,</i>

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.470
<i>and the weather changes completely.</i>

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.950
I can go.
I can go today.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.780
Grandfather, I can go today, can't I?

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.670
Grandmother has been expecting me for days.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.730
Heidi, you can't go today.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.810
It stopped snowing
and it's a beautiful day.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.540
It's all quiet.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:53.760
It stopped snowing, but the snow is
too deep now so we can't go yet.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.790
I finished.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.280
All right, come along then.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:28.930
Where are we going?

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.350
To Peter's house.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:34.390
The snow has frozen during the night,

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.420
so we can go today.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.750
Really, we can go now?

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:58.450
How beautiful!

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.450
Grandfather!

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.240
What is it?

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.870
Look! The fir trees are all silver and gold!

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:26.760
What is that?

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:28.160
A sleigh.

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:29.230
A sleigh?

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:29.900
Yes.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.510
Are we going in that?

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.070
Yes, it's faster this way.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:35.110
Really?

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:36.110
Come on, get in.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:37.110
Yes.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.260
Are you cold?

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.550
No, I'm all right.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.450
Listen, hold on to me tightly
so you don't fall off.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:47.450
All right.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:48.770
Then, let's go.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.340
Here we go!

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.560
It's so fast!
It's almost as if we're flying.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.160
Is this Peter's house?

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:39.160
Yes, it is.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.970
Well, you must come back before it gets dark.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.570
You won't come with me?

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:54.620
Did you understand?

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:56.180
Yes.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:56.740
Is that you, Brigitte?

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.770
No, I am Heidi.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:08.810
Good day, Grandmother. I have come at last.
Did you think I was a long time coming?

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.100
Are you the child who lives up with Alm-Uncle?

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:19.090
Are you Heidi?

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.400
Yes, that's right.
I'm Heidi.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:27.200
Oh my! Your hands are quite warm!

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.130
Grandmother, you have to fix your shutter quickly,

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.960
or the glass will break.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:45.500
That's true. But I am so glad
that you are here, Heidi.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:49.840
But look, Grandmother,
it is flapping back and forth.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:57.510
Yes, yes. But I can't see it.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:05.510
You can see it.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:06.640
No.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.340
But I can hear it.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:12.780
And not just the sound of the shutters.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:17.210
Everything about the place rattles
and creaks when the wind is blowing.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:21.420
I don't know when the
wind carries rain or snow,

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:23.720
but I can hear it.

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:36.160
And in the night, I sometimes think
the house is about to fall down,

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.970
and that all of us will be killed.

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:44.550
But why can't you see that
the shutter is loose, Grandmother?

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.620
Look, there it goes again.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:51.390
Oh, the shutters aren't the
only things I can't see.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.140
I can't see anything.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.040
Then I'll open the windows completely.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.560
Then you will see, won't you?

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.570
No. I still wouldn't be able to see.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:08.930
No one can make it light for me again.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.840
But if you were to go outside
among all the white snow,

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.130
then surely you would find it light, right?

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.100
Just come with me, Grandmother.
Let's go, Grandmother.

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:34.290
You're such a kind child,

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:37.250
but I'd rather stay here.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.460
Don't say that.
Come with me.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.650
It's fine, Heidi. It's no use.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:51.420
My eyes will never see again.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:56.450
Even in snow and sunlight,
it's always in dark for me.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:57.450
But...

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:01.040
But surely it does in summer, when the hot sun is

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.800
shining down again, and he says good-night
to the mountains, and they all turn on fire,

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.310
and lots of yellow flowers
shine, then you will see,

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.470
it will be bright and beautiful
for you again, won't it?

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.800
Thank you, Heidi.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:20.970
But I shall never see the mountains on fire
or the yellow flowers any more.

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:27.810
It will never be light for
me again in this world.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:50.910
Heidi.

00:11:58.700 --> 00:11:59.970
Heidi.

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.490
Isn't there anyone who can make
it light for you again?

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:10.090
Can no one do it? No one at all?

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.260
No.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:21.740
It's all right, Heidi.
You don't have to worry.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:30.720
You cannot think how glad one is
to hear a kind word when one can no longer see.

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:35.300
Now don't cry anymore and
tell me about yourself.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:40.990
<i>Heidi rarely cries,</i>

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:45.860
<i>but this time she just keeps on crying.</i>

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.000
Heidi.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:00.060
Wait, Grandmother, till I have
told Grandfather everything.

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:04.040
He will make it light for you again, I am sure.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:08.180
He'll fix the shutters, and make
sure that the house won't fall.

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:11.950
Grandfather can fix anything!

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.550
Thank you, Heidi.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:18.010
You really are a kind child.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:21.610
Is that you, Brigitte?

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:23.270
<i>Yes.</i>

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:24.930
Come here.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:28.460
Heidi is here.
Alm-Uncle's granddaughter.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:30.590
Good day, Aunty.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:36.950
What is this child like, Brigitte? Please tell me.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.680
Let me see.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.780
She's pretty like Adelheid, her mother,

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.140
but she has dark eyes
and curly hair like her father.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:50.920
Her father Tobias looked just like Alm-Uncle.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.830
She takes after both of them, I think.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.000
Is that so?

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.840
But I am so happy that you came.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:02.130
Wasn't it dangerous with the snow?

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:05.920
No. I came down in the
sleigh with Grandfather.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.320
Oh, is that true?

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.740
Did Alm-Uncle really bring you here?

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:15.840
That's right, in a sleigh.
It felt like we were flying.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.450
It was so fast!

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:22.640
It can't be.

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:25.400
The child is probably mistaken.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:29.490
I can't believe that Uncle has become so nice.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:31.710
I'm not lying!

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.550
Grandfather really brought me here.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:42.520
Then, what Peter told us wasn't a lie.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:43.830
Peter?

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:48.270
Yes, he said that Alm-Uncle really likes you.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:49.930
Yes, he does.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:57.250
I find it hard to believe that about him.

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:01.720
Who would ever have believed that
such a thing was possible?

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.120
Well, Heidi,

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:08.570
What you do up there on the mountain?

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:11.000
Peter didn't tell you?

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:14.710
He never says much.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:16.950
I will tell you everything.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:22.300
<i>Heidi begins to talk about her
life with Grandfather,</i>

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:26.060
<i>since she came to live on the mountain.</i>

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:30.160
<i>And she talks about her life
during the winter months.</i>

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.270
And my grandfather is able
to make all sorts of things.

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:41.800
Seats and stools, they are made
so magically out of pieces of wood.

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:43.210
He also makes toys for me.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:45.640
Do you hear that, Brigitta?

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.490
Yes, I did. To think that
Uncle has become so kind...

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.110
<i>Really.</i>

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:57.090
You two have been
saying that all afternoon.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:15:58.900
<i>I'm home!</i>

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.990
Welcome back, Peter!

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.640
Heidi! So you came.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:10.200
Oh my, you're back already, Peter?

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:11.790
Where were you?

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:12.790
In school.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:18.560
I have not known an afternoon pass
so quickly as this one for years.

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:22.900
How is the reading getting on, Peter?

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:25.880
Just the same.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.860
Ah, well.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:33.830
Peter, you are going to be twelve
years old this February.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.820
I had hoped you would have
something different to tell me by this time.

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:43.940
What was it that you hoped
he would have to tell you?

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:49.500
I mean that he ought to have
learnt to read a bit by now.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:54.390
Up there on the shelf is an old prayer-book.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:59.090
That book has many beautiful songs in it,

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:03.330
which I have not heard for a long time,

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:07.920
and have forgotten completely.

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:12.770
And I hoped that Peter
would soon learn enough

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:17.880
to be able to read one of those
beautiful songs to me sometimes.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:19.920
Text from <i>"Entwurf eines Gesangbuches für
die evangelische Kirche im Königreich
Württemberg"</i> from 1840, page 319,
song 451, verses 8 & 9.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:19.990
But,

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:27.450
Peter can't read well.
So he can't read them to me.

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:31.920
That book is too difficult for me.

00:17:31.930 --> 00:17:33.440
Is that so?

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:56.050
What's wrong, Heidi?
Don't you have more to tell us?

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:58.300
I have to go home.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:02.110
I promised Grandfather to go home
before it got dark.

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:04.350
Oh, it's that time already?

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:09.900
It's become evening before I knew it.

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.250
Heidi, will you come again?

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:13.660
Yes, I will come again.

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:18.090
Good night, Grandmother.
It will be bad if I don't go home soon.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.380
I hope you will be back soon, Heidi.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:22.380
Yes.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:25.500
Wait, Heidi.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:26.500
What is it, Grandmother?

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.250
Did you bring a thick shawl?

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:31.280
No, I didn't.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:32.280
Peter.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:33.280
Yes.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:35.810
Give this to Heidi.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:40.580
Don't worry, Grandmother.
I am sure I shall not be cold.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.000
Heidi!

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:48.470
Peter, follow Heidi outside.
Make sure she doesn't fall.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.470
All right.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:50.950
Thank you, Grandmother.

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:54.740
Goodbye!

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:56.740
Be careful!

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:03.710
Grandfather!

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:08.550
That's right, Heidi.
You have kept your word.

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:09.550
Yes.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:10.560
Peter!

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:13.040
Return this to your grandmother.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:14.040
Yes.

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:20.070
General, I'll see you soon.

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:21.630
Goodbye.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:22.960
Goodbye.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:25.700
Goodbye!

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.810
Goodbye!

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.460
Mother!

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:40.070
Alm-Uncle came to get the girl.

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:41.910
Alm-Uncle did?

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:43.140
Yes!

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.570
Is that so?

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:51.370
That's great. He's so good to Heidi.

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:54.650
Haven't I been saying that all this time?

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:58.050
Well, that's true.
Right, mother?

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:02.540
I wonder if she will come to me again.

00:20:02.950 --> 00:20:06.920
Now I have something to
look forward to in this world.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.420
You know what, Grandfather?

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:14.100
What is it?

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:20.200
Tomorrow we must take the hammer and
the long nails and fasten Grandmother's shutters.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:22.050
What?

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:26.050
And there are lots of other places that needs
mending, apart from the shutters.

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:29.930
Really?
We must, must we?

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.630
Who told you that?

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:37.630
Nobody told me,
I decided to ask you myself.

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:43.560
Because you can do everything, right?
Let's go back tomorrow.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:15.010
Ah, my God, now it is coming,
the house is going to fall upon us!

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:16.650
Grandmother, good day.

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:17.650
Heidi!

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:19.390
What's wrong, Grandmother?

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:24.840
Heidi, this is the end!
Everything is falling apart!

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.120
Grandmother, do not be frightened.

00:21:30.740 --> 00:21:33.430
That's just the sound of
my grandfather striking the nails.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:34.430
What?

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:35.430
Alm-Uncle?

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:38.200
But, why is he here?

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:42.350
Don't you remember?
Yesterday, I promised we'd come back to make repairs.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:43.350
Heidi...

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:50.420
Brigitte!
So God has not forgotten us!

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:51.420
That's right.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:59.300
Now, as I listen, I can tell
it is the sound of a hammer.

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:01.790
Brigitte,

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:07.080
please tell Uncle to come inside
a moment, so I may thank him.

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:08.080
All right.

00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:18.910
Good evening, Alm-Uncle.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:25.300
We have to thank you
for doing us such a kind service.

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:32.570
Mother would like to tell you herself
how grateful she is, f-for I am sure...

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:34.100
That will do!

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:35.100
All right.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:40.260
I know what you think of me
without your telling me.

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:45.840
Have you come to check
up on this bad man? Go back inside.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:47.680
Yes.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:55.260
<i>The way Grandfather speaks
is part of his character,</i>

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:57.750
<i>and Brigitte knows that.</i>

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:01.110
<i>And that day,</i>

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:05.340
<i>Grandfather goes all around
the house and up on the roof</i>

00:23:05.770 --> 00:23:08.910
<i>to mend everything that is broken.</i>

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:25.820
Goodbye!

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:31.180
<i>For Grandmother, who hasn't had anything
pleasant happen to her in a long time,</i>

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:34.790
<i>this day is the happiest ever.</i>

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:37.980
<i>And even though she can't see,</i>

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:46.420
<i>the golden light of sunset over the mountains
shines brightly inside Grandmother's heart.</i>

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:58.430
<i>On Alm Mountain, a violent snowstorm
has raged for many days.</i>

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:01.810
<i>Even so, Heidi plays with Peter,</i>

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:05.630
<i>and feed the animals who gather
under the tree during the storm.</i>

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:08.500
<i>They pass each day in a happy mood.</i>

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.610
<i>One day, when the snow stops falling,</i>

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:13.610
<i>two hunters come up the mountain to hunt.</i>

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:17.890
<i>They don't listen to the old man, and keep
going deep into the mountains,</i>

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:21.360
<i>and are finally trapped in a snowstorm.</i>

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.680
<i>Heidi becomes very worried for them.</i>

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:27.410
<i>Next episode:
The Snowstorm</i>

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:29.030
<i>Please look forward to it!</i>

